#slide()[
#text(size: 30pt, weight: "bold")[Representativeness Heuristic]
#v(40pt)
#only("1-")[- Judging probability based on similarity 🤔]
#v(20pt)
#only("2-")[- Example: Linda the bank teller 👩‍💼]
#v(20pt)
#only("3-")[- Ignores base rates and sample size 📊]
#only("1")[
#voiceover("The representativeness heuristic is a mental shortcut where people judge the probability of an event based on how much it resembles or is similar to another event or stereotype.")
]
#only("2")[
#voiceover("For example, in the famous 'Linda problem', people are told about Linda, who is described as outspoken and concerned with social justice. When asked if it's more likely that Linda is a bank teller, or a bank teller and active in the feminist movement, most people choose the latter. This is because the description of Linda is more 'representative' of a feminist bank teller, even though logically, the probability of two events occurring together is always less than or equal to the probability of either event occurring alone.")  
]
#only("3")[
#voiceover("The representativeness heuristic often leads to neglect of relevant base rates and sample sizes. People tend to focus on the specific information provided and ignore the general statistical data.")
]
]

#slide()[
#text(size: 30pt, weight: "bold")[Gambler's Fallacy]
#v(40pt)
#only("1-")[- Belief that past events affect future probabilities 🎲]
#v(20pt)
#only("2-")[- Example: Roulette wheel 🎰]
#only("1")[
#voiceover("One manifestation of the representativeness heuristic is the gambler's fallacy. This is the belief that if something happens more frequently than normal during a given period, it will happen less frequently in the future, or vice versa.")
]
#only("2")[
#voiceover("For instance, if a roulette wheel lands on black several times in a row, gamblers might incorrectly believe that red is 'due', and that the probability of the next spin landing on red is higher than black. In reality, each spin is an independent event, and the probability of red or black is always the same, regardless of past spins.")
]  
]

#slide()[
#text(size: 30pt, weight: "bold")[Overcoming the Bias]
#v(40pt)
#only("1-")[- Be aware of the bias 🧠]
#v(20pt)  
#only("2-")[- Consider base rates and sample sizes 📏]
#v(20pt)
#only("3-")[- Understand probability theory 🎓]
#only("1")[
#voiceover("To overcome the representativeness heuristic, the first step is to be aware of the bias and consciously look out for it in our thinking.")
]
#only("2")[
#voiceover("When making judgments, we should consider the relevant base rates and sample sizes, not just how representative something seems.")
]
#only("3")[
#voiceover("A solid understanding of probability theory can also help counter intuitive but incorrect judgments. By relying on statistical principles instead of gut feelings, we can make more accurate assessments of likelihood.")
]
]

#slide()[
#text(size: 30pt, weight: "bold")[Summary]
#v(40pt)  
